1
00:00:23,977 --> 00:00:30,006
Hello and welcome to the NPTEL MOOCs course on Foundations of Virtual and Augmented Reality Systems.

2
00:00:31,048 --> 00:00:43,987
We are currently discussing aspects of system design, and we have already covered a few topics, and we are going to cover a few more in this part of the lecture.

3
00:00:45,549 --> 00:00:53,241
So, essentially, we are talking about how to design immersive experiences, and in that context, we have already talked about

4
00:00:54,706 --> 00:00:57,069
interaction techniques and their importance.

5
00:00:59,232 --> 00:01:04,178
We have covered one interaction technique, that is, object interaction.

6
00:01:04,818 --> 00:01:12,528
So, if you can recollect, there are different types of interactions that we encounter in an immersive experience.

7
00:01:15,371 --> 00:01:23,481
Broadly, they are of three types: object interaction, navigation, and system control, out of which we have covered object interaction, which includes

8
00:01:23,866 --> 00:01:26,268
selection and manipulation interaction.

9
00:01:27,349 --> 00:01:38,860
We are going to continue our discussion on interaction techniques in this part of the lecture as well, where we will be covering the other components of interaction or other types of interactions.

10
00:01:41,543 --> 00:01:42,804
Now, what are the interaction types?

11
00:01:43,084 --> 00:01:51,612
Just a quick recap: we have object interaction, then navigation, and finally, interaction for system control.

12
00:01:51,895 --> 00:01:56,742
Out of which, object interaction is already taken care of in the previous part of the lecture.

13
00:01:58,204 --> 00:02:02,870
Remaining are navigation and system control, interaction for system control.

14
00:02:03,051 --> 00:02:08,258
We are going to cover these two, the remaining types of interactions, in this lecture.

15
00:02:12,103 --> 00:02:21,156
So, what are the different navigation techniques that are typically used in any VR setting or immersive setting?

16
00:02:21,176 --> 00:02:21,717
Let us have a

17
00:02:22,085 --> 00:02:22,646
Look at it.

18
00:02:23,486 --> 00:02:26,369
Now, when we talk about navigation, what is the goal of navigation?

19
00:02:26,950 --> 00:02:32,636
The goal is to move the user from one point to another in the virtual world.

20
00:02:34,798 --> 00:02:40,544
That means a change of the user's field of view or viewpoint.

21
00:02:41,005 --> 00:02:44,028
I would say a change of the user's viewpoint.

22
00:02:44,048 --> 00:02:48,633
Now, there are actually two parts in any such movement.

23
00:02:49,934 --> 00:02:50,995
One is the travel part.

24
00:02:52,223 --> 00:02:58,250
And the other is called the wayfinding part.

25
00:02:58,270 --> 00:03:04,950
Now the travel part is also known as the locomotion and

26
00:03:04,950 --> 00:03:16,411
the corresponding techniques that are used to let the user travel in a virtual world from one place to another are called locomotion techniques or alternatively travel techniques.

27
00:03:16,431 --> 00:03:20,155
Now, travel is the motor component of navigation.

28
00:03:20,776 --> 00:03:28,187
That is the actual movement when we actually move our legs or limbs to move from one point to another.

29
00:03:30,470 --> 00:03:40,244
That is the motor component of movement that we generally refer to as the travel or locomotion part of navigation.

30
00:03:41,305 --> 00:03:47,374
So, essentially it refers to the movement between two locations, from one point to another.

31
00:03:50,071 --> 00:03:57,247
Now, there are different techniques employed to let the user perceive that travel has taken place.

32
00:03:59,512 --> 00:04:01,577
Travel can be done in different ways.

33
00:04:03,341 --> 00:04:04,544
One is exploration.

34
00:04:06,067 --> 00:04:11,434
It means that there is no explicit goal to reach.

35
00:04:11,434 --> 00:04:15,575
I mean there is no specific point where the user wants to reach.

36
00:04:15,995 --> 00:04:20,801
The user instead just wants to move around, to see things, to learn things, to explore things.

37
00:04:21,522 --> 00:04:23,705
So that is the exploration type of travel.

38
00:04:25,326 --> 00:04:26,968
Then we have search.

39
00:04:27,950 --> 00:04:31,654
Searching for a specified target location.

40
00:04:32,883 --> 00:04:35,546
Or moving to a specified target location.

41
00:04:35,586 --> 00:04:43,315
So, the user now, instead of exploration, wants to move to a specific location that comes under this type of travel.

42
00:04:47,439 --> 00:04:49,341
The third technique is maneuvering.

43
00:04:50,823 --> 00:04:57,330
It essentially refers to short, precise movements changing the point of view or the viewpoint.

44
00:04:57,971 --> 00:05:01,455
So, when we are traveling from one point to another,

45
00:05:01,958 --> 00:05:17,321
Either we can do the traveling at one go, or we can travel as a combination of smaller, short, specific, focused travels.

46
00:05:17,882 --> 00:05:30,902
That means a path is broken down into small segments, and travel between each segment is performed so that at the end we reach the destination.

47
00:05:31,439 --> 00:05:44,022
So, this type of movement where the overall movement comprises short precise smaller movements is called maneuvering.

48
00:05:44,042 --> 00:05:54,021
So, we can have different ways to travel from one point to another; now these traveling

49
00:05:54,440 --> 00:06:06,477
these traveling types can be accomplished using four broad types of locomotion techniques.

50
00:06:06,497 --> 00:06:24,261
We can have locomotion based on walking, that is walking-based locomotion, then steering-based locomotion, then selection-based locomotion, and finally, manipulation-based locomotion.

51
00:06:24,612 --> 00:06:36,095
Remember in our earlier part of the discussion we said that object interaction and navigation are different in the sense that in object interaction typically,

52
00:06:36,095 --> 00:06:41,637
manipulation of objects is involved whereas in navigation that is not generally the case.

53
00:06:42,839 --> 00:06:45,383
Object manipulation may not be involved.

54
00:06:46,805 --> 00:06:51,773
That is true for the first three types of locomotion techniques.

55
00:06:51,793 --> 00:06:53,455
However, in the last technique

56
00:06:54,194 --> 00:07:01,766
we do navigation by manipulating certain things that we will discuss at the appropriate time.

57
00:07:02,327 --> 00:07:11,040
So, manipulation is not entirely avoided while implementing travel techniques in an immersive setting.

58
00:07:11,841 --> 00:07:17,991
However, mostly navigation involves no manipulation of objects.

59
00:07:21,937 --> 00:07:27,545
Now, let us try to understand each of these techniques one by one.

60
00:07:28,967 --> 00:07:34,255
We will start with the first technique, which is walking-based locomotion.

61
00:07:34,275 --> 00:07:45,252
As the name suggests, walking-based locomotion refers to traveling in the virtual world by physically walking in the physical world or the real world.

62
00:07:46,313 --> 00:07:50,239
So, some sort of walking or some sort of movement, physical movement is involved

63
00:07:50,557 --> 00:07:53,882
For corresponding virtual movement in the virtual world.

64
00:07:54,442 --> 00:08:01,091
Now, this type of locomotion can further be divided into several categories, broadly three categories.

65
00:08:01,512 --> 00:08:08,682
One is full gait technique, partial gait techniques and gait negation techniques.

66
00:08:09,203 --> 00:08:19,737
So, these three are the broad categories of walking-based locomotion techniques: full gait, partial gait and gait negation techniques.

67
00:08:20,695 --> 00:08:22,658
What happens in full gait techniques?

68
00:08:23,539 --> 00:08:25,922
That actually involves real walking.

69
00:08:26,864 --> 00:08:42,164
So the user actually walks in the real world and that walk gets translated to virtual movement in the virtual environment, in the immersive environment.

70
00:08:42,184 --> 00:08:45,530
Obviously, this is the most natural way to travel.

71
00:08:45,770 --> 00:08:49,335
However, as we have seen earlier,

72
00:08:50,058 --> 00:08:58,890
giving a completely free form of walking is not practical or possible for virtual navigation.

73
00:08:58,910 --> 00:09:16,412
Instead, the walking is restricted within a specified limit in the physical space, which then gets translated to any amount of movement in the potentially infinite virtual space.

74
00:09:16,432 --> 00:09:19,997
So, of course, there is a mismatch, although this provides

75
00:09:20,720 --> 00:09:24,985
natural form of interaction for navigation as compared to the other techniques.

76
00:09:29,491 --> 00:09:33,916
Then comes the second technique, that is partial gait technique.

77
00:09:35,799 --> 00:09:42,327
Unlike the previous full gait technique, here partial gait refers to the fact that the user walks but walks in place.

78
00:09:45,090 --> 00:09:48,094
That means the user does not actually change the position.

79
00:09:48,580 --> 00:10:00,781
As shown in this figure here, as you can see in this animated figure, the user is walking but in a single place.

80
00:10:01,302 --> 00:10:04,127
It is not actually moving forward, backward, left, or right.

81
00:10:06,010 --> 00:10:07,572
That is the partial gait technique.

82
00:10:08,955 --> 00:10:12,040
Now, implementation of these types of techniques may involve

83
00:10:13,117 --> 00:10:23,331
walking interfaces, active walking interfaces such as pressure mats, pressure sensors and/or interfaces with pressure sensors and so on.

84
00:10:23,932 --> 00:10:30,040
So that depending on the walking behavior, although it may be in a single place,

85
00:10:30,040 --> 00:10:38,753
but depending on the behavior of walking in that single place, the pattern can be mapped to different types of walking in the virtual space.

86
00:10:41,163 --> 00:10:51,372
So, partial gait techniques require some sort of specialized devices in addition to other immersive hardware for providing input.

87
00:10:51,392 --> 00:10:58,832
Now, in case of gait negation technique, even more specialized setup is required because in such technique,

88
00:10:58,832 --> 00:11:09,988
the user's lateral movements are canceled due to the specified or specialized setup used, keeping their physical position fixed.

89
00:11:11,183 --> 00:11:26,778
So, although the user may be moving in multiple directions, the lateral movements in that movement pattern get negated,

90
00:11:26,778 --> 00:11:38,423
get canceled because of the specialized setup used and the user or the system maps that movement

91
00:11:38,960 --> 00:11:42,786
to a specialized or specific type of virtual movement.

92
00:11:46,311 --> 00:11:47,873
So, the user may be walking naturally.

93
00:11:48,614 --> 00:11:51,759
However, natural movement involves lateral movement.

94
00:11:53,561 --> 00:12:02,815
But to map that type of absolutely natural movements to a particular type of focused movement in the virtual world may be difficult.

95
00:12:03,396 --> 00:12:05,659
So, a specialized setup is used to

96
00:12:06,888 --> 00:12:17,557
Cancel the lateral movements of the user so that it is easier for the system to understand the user's intention and accordingly perform the navigation in the virtual world.

97
00:12:18,959 --> 00:12:20,740
So, what kind of specialized setup is used?

98
00:12:21,140 --> 00:12:24,323
So, for example, a linear treadmill may be used.

99
00:12:26,185 --> 00:12:29,668
Motorized floor tiles may be used.

100
00:12:32,350 --> 00:12:36,574
A human-sized hamster ball may be used.

101
00:12:37,668 --> 00:12:41,452
Or a friction-free platform may be used.

102
00:12:42,072 --> 00:12:43,133
These are some of the techniques.

103
00:12:43,173 --> 00:12:46,577
Of course, there can be other setups possible.

104
00:12:47,718 --> 00:13:02,413
Now, using this type of specialized setup, whatever lateral movement takes place can be detected and eliminated before the movement is mapped to the virtual navigation.

105
00:13:02,433 --> 00:13:06,797
Now, all these are part of walking-based navigation.

106
00:13:07,081 --> 00:13:09,985
The second category of navigation is steering-based.

107
00:13:14,471 --> 00:13:21,440
It indicates continuous control of direction and speed while the user is moving from one point to another.

108
00:13:23,243 --> 00:13:34,017
So, whenever there is a continuous control of direction and speed, essentially we can safely assume that the system has implemented steering-based navigation.

109
00:13:35,161 --> 00:13:41,887
Now, this is also of two types.

110
00:13:41,907 --> 00:13:43,809
Spatial steering-based navigation.

111
00:13:44,009 --> 00:13:57,030
In this case, the navigation is controlled using the user's body parts and physical steering-based navigation, where the navigation is controlled;

112
00:13:57,030 --> 00:14:02,907
the locomotion is controlled using vehicular props or some additional hardware.

113
00:14:07,021 --> 00:14:12,009
What is the spatial steering-based locomotion technique?

114
00:14:12,029 --> 00:14:17,457
So, it can be gaze-directed, that is, the travel follows where the user is looking.

115
00:14:17,797 --> 00:14:24,347
So, just by looking at a distance, we can move continuously to that target location.

116
00:14:24,828 --> 00:14:28,533
So, it follows the direction of looking.

117
00:14:30,757 --> 00:14:32,079
Then it can be hand-directed.

118
00:14:33,020 --> 00:14:35,103
Hand direction determines direction of travel.

119
00:14:35,143 --> 00:14:36,285
So, we can

120
00:14:37,463 --> 00:14:46,918
provide the direction through hand gestures, and then correspondingly the system maps those hand gestures to travel in a specified direction where the hand is pointing.

121
00:14:49,502 --> 00:14:51,305
It can also be body-leaning directed.

122
00:14:51,385 --> 00:14:59,337
So, we lean the body toward the direction of movement, and then that gets mapped and then corresponding travel takes place.

123
00:15:04,092 --> 00:15:10,546
As opposed to that, where we are using our body parts like gaze, eye, or hand, or whole

124
00:15:10,546 --> 00:15:22,292
body, there is a physical steering-based locomotion technique category as well, which involves some physical setups, some separate

125
00:15:22,292 --> 00:15:33,728
hardware such as bike locomotion platforms, VR simulators for aircraft, merchant ships, cars, boats, spaceships.

126
00:15:34,062 --> 00:15:40,553
These actually come with this type of physical steering as shown in this example.

127
00:15:40,653 --> 00:15:51,090
So, this is a bike simulator where you can see the physical steering, and by moving the steering physically, you can perform the travel in the virtual world.

128
00:15:52,312 --> 00:16:00,205
Similarly, here you have physical steering props which are used to

129
00:16:00,877 --> 00:16:09,933
In this flight simulator here, which is used to perform travel in the virtual world.

130
00:16:16,303 --> 00:16:18,748
So that is about walking-based and steering-based.

131
00:16:18,848 --> 00:16:24,357
The third category is selection-based navigation or locomotion technique.

132
00:16:24,377 --> 00:16:25,399
Now it allows

133
00:16:26,240 --> 00:16:30,005
users to focus on where to go rather than how to.

134
00:16:30,025 --> 00:16:50,273
In the previous techniques, user has to specify by moving the steering or looking at the direction continuously or performing work for movement; user has to specify how to

135
00:16:50,692 --> 00:16:59,505
reach a destination, whereas in case of selection-based, user can focus on where to go, need to select where to go rather than specify how to go there.

136
00:17:01,588 --> 00:17:08,117
Now this locomotion technique category can also be of different types.

137
00:17:09,960 --> 00:17:18,571
We can have target selection-based locomotion; we can have route planning-based locomotion.

138
00:17:19,311 --> 00:17:29,443
In case of target selection-based locomotion, what happens is that the user selects the destination or the target where the user wants to move.

139
00:17:29,463 --> 00:17:32,206
And the virtual viewpoint changes.

140
00:17:32,987 --> 00:17:41,436
Or in other words, it lets the user feel through that change that the user has actually moved to the target.

141
00:17:41,456 --> 00:17:45,060
One very common such technique is teleportation.

142
00:17:45,080 --> 00:17:48,103
That is instantly moving target.

143
00:17:48,691 --> 00:17:51,675
from current location to the target location.

144
00:17:51,695 --> 00:17:57,323
Similarly, with map dragging, if we fix the location, then we can instantly move there.

145
00:17:58,144 --> 00:18:03,371
Another technique is the jumper technique in cave-type systems.

146
00:18:06,275 --> 00:18:17,871
So, as shown in this small animation, the user here is selecting the target and then immediately the viewpoint changes.

147
00:18:18,610 --> 00:18:26,598
As if there is an instant travel taking place from the current viewpoint to the destination viewpoint.

148
00:18:27,699 --> 00:18:29,641
This is an example of teleportation.

149
00:18:33,985 --> 00:18:39,151
The next type of technique is route planning.

150
00:18:39,811 --> 00:18:46,131
This is also another type of selection-based technique.

151
00:18:46,131 --> 00:18:51,210
However, in this case, it is not that only the end is selected.

152
00:18:53,033 --> 00:19:04,930
It can provide more granular control over travel, unlike teleportation or target-based locomotion where only the endpoint is selected and we instantly move there.

153
00:19:05,691 --> 00:19:12,580
In the case of route planning, what happens is that instead of the target, we select a route that takes us to the target.

154
00:19:12,600 --> 00:19:13,862
There may be multiple routes.

155
00:19:14,888 --> 00:19:24,736
And the user selects a route that takes them to the target in the virtual world, and while moving through that route, different viewpoint changes are implemented so

156
00:19:24,736 --> 00:19:33,997
that the user feels they are moving along a path.

157
00:19:36,320 --> 00:19:43,731
So, it is a slightly more natural kind of movement as compared to target-based

158
00:19:44,318 --> 00:19:46,020
teleportation-type movement.

159
00:19:51,465 --> 00:19:53,508
So those are about selection-based.

160
00:19:53,568 --> 00:20:01,996
The final category of locomotion techniques is manipulation-based locomotion techniques.

161
00:20:02,016 --> 00:20:14,089
Here the locomotion works by manipulating the user's position, orientation, or scale using gestures that control either the virtual viewpoint or the virtual world.

162
00:20:17,849 --> 00:20:24,917
Earlier we have talked about the go-go technique, homer technique, and world in miniature metaphor.

163
00:20:25,518 --> 00:20:36,491
So, using a combination of these different manipulation techniques and metaphors, we can implement manipulation-based locomotion.

164
00:20:38,453 --> 00:20:42,658
Say for example, in a world in miniature metaphor, if we move

165
00:20:43,297 --> 00:20:53,790
certain objects, then correspondingly or manipulate certain objects, then corresponding change in viewpoint happens as if the user is moving from one place to another.

166
00:20:54,131 --> 00:20:59,678
So, this type of implementation is possible through manipulation-based locomotion techniques.

167
00:20:59,698 --> 00:21:09,450
Since we have already discussed these techniques before, we are not going to discuss further what is go-go or what is homer manipulation or what is world in miniature.

168
00:21:09,791 --> 00:21:11,633
Please refer to the previous discussions.

169
00:21:13,233 --> 00:21:16,116
Previous lectures for discussion on these concepts.

170
00:21:18,719 --> 00:21:23,063
So, with that we have come to the end of our discussion on locomotion techniques.

171
00:21:23,083 --> 00:21:32,013
Now, if you recollect the other component of locomotion is wayfinding; the other component of navigation is wayfinding.

172
00:21:32,033 --> 00:21:38,059
So, there are two components of navigation: one is locomotion or travel, that is the motor component and

173
00:21:38,393 --> 00:21:40,455
The second component is wayfinding.

174
00:21:40,956 --> 00:21:43,719
So far we have discussed the motor component.

175
00:21:43,879 --> 00:21:51,327
Now, let us move our attention to the wayfinding component.

176
00:21:51,347 --> 00:22:03,861
Now, wayfinding refers to many things such as location awareness where one is located, and ascertaining a path through the environment to the desired destination.

177
00:22:06,203 --> 00:22:09,367
transferring spatial knowledge to the real world.

178
00:22:14,054 --> 00:22:19,181
So, essentially it refers to a mental model.

179
00:22:19,241 --> 00:22:27,843
So, the goal of wayfinding is basically to build a mental model or a cognitive map of the surroundings as well as the overall environment so that

180
00:22:27,843 --> 00:22:35,063
it is easier for us to locate ourselves in the environment as well as to identify a suitable path

181
00:22:35,566 --> 00:22:38,409
to take us to the destination.

182
00:22:38,429 --> 00:22:49,502
Now our objective should be to design the environment in a way such that it is easier for the user to build such a mental map.

183
00:22:50,683 --> 00:23:04,459
If it is missing, if the design does not support creation of this mental map then a lot of problems may happen and that will have a deteriorating effect on the overall user experience.

184
00:23:07,139 --> 00:23:26,966
Now, when we talk of spatial knowledge in that mental model or the cognitive map, what we are referring to is landmark knowledge, procedural knowledge and topological or map-like knowledge.

185
00:23:26,986 --> 00:23:34,075
So, we should have knowledge about landmarks in our surroundings as well as in other places

186
00:23:36,383 --> 00:23:47,998
some sort of procedural knowledge or the sequence of actions required to follow a path and overall map of the environment or topological knowledge of the environment.

187
00:23:48,018 --> 00:23:56,869
Together they constitute the knowledge base which is required or which is an essential part of the overall mental model.

188
00:23:59,132 --> 00:24:01,314
Now how can we create the mental model?

189
00:24:01,755 --> 00:24:05,800
Essentially by aiding the user

190
00:24:08,125 --> 00:24:15,316
to get the knowledge, to understand the knowledge or to infer the knowledge.

191
00:24:15,336 --> 00:24:32,101
So essentially this can be done by the traditional methods: systematic study of the map of a place, exploration of real space, exploration of a copy of the real space, or a model of the real space.

192
00:24:32,121 --> 00:24:35,466
Either method or a combination of all may give us

193
00:24:37,724 --> 00:24:40,849
the mental model or the cognitive model.

194
00:24:43,874 --> 00:24:56,874
Then what is required for the system to provide a good user experience, immersive user experience, is some sort of support for wayfinding.

195
00:24:57,295 --> 00:25:02,623
Note that it is not sufficient to provide good travel support only.

196
00:25:03,104 --> 00:25:05,908
Along with that, we need to provide good wayfinding support

197
00:25:06,968 --> 00:25:15,381
so that the travel supports are effectively used or deployed by the user while performing navigation.

198
00:25:15,401 --> 00:25:20,051
So without suitable wayfinding support in a complex environment, irrespective

199
00:25:20,051 --> 00:25:28,180
of how many different types of traveling techniques are implemented, the user will not be able to perform efficient movement.

200
00:25:29,362 --> 00:25:32,086
So how can we provide wayfinding support?

201
00:25:32,106 --> 00:25:35,912
Say one way is to have landmarks.

202
00:25:38,339 --> 00:25:45,347
These are any obvious distinct non-mobile objects that are positioned in a given place.

203
00:25:47,470 --> 00:25:50,033
A good landmark can be seen from several locations.

204
00:25:50,093 --> 00:25:59,344
So it should be clearly visible from different points of view, different locations.

205
00:25:59,364 --> 00:26:04,109
We can also provide support for maps of the space, of the place.

206
00:26:06,272 --> 00:26:08,194
It can be a copy of the world map.

207
00:26:09,980 --> 00:26:16,928
In fact, here the term world refers to the virtual world, not the real world, or it can be a world in miniature.

208
00:26:18,931 --> 00:26:25,341
So, for example, here it shows a world in miniature type of

209
00:26:25,341 --> 00:26:37,253
Wayfinding support which shows the entire world with the objects located at different points, and it can also show the user's current position.

210
00:26:37,570 --> 00:26:41,315
and different ways to reach different points, many things.

211
00:26:46,903 --> 00:26:52,699
Another way the wayfinding can be supported is through path following; that is,

212
00:26:52,699 --> 00:27:04,868
multiple paths through a single space may be denoted by colors, so that the user knows that there are different paths to reach a destination.

213
00:27:04,888 --> 00:27:06,430
Breadcrumbs can provide

214
00:27:07,608 --> 00:27:16,216
Another kind of support, it is essentially the idea to leave a trail of the path followed by predecessors.

215
00:27:16,916 --> 00:27:23,122
So, leaving a trail of markers allows the users to know when they have been somewhere before.

216
00:27:23,583 --> 00:27:27,946
So, that the user does not get lost; the user knows that this path is explored.

217
00:27:27,967 --> 00:27:36,214
So, let us move to a different path or different destination to save time and so on.

218
00:27:42,488 --> 00:27:50,343
A simple aid can be a compass; another form of direction indicator can specify directions in both 2D or 3D space.

219
00:27:50,984 --> 00:27:55,152
So, accordingly, a compass can be designed and placed in the environment.

220
00:27:55,493 --> 00:27:57,997
So, there can be different ways to support wayfinding.

221
00:27:58,578 --> 00:28:04,750
The most crucial thing is to remember that support for wayfinding is

222
00:28:05,000 --> 00:28:15,716
an essential requirement if you are designing a complex virtual environment with lots of objects and different ways to reach the targets.

223
00:28:17,198 --> 00:28:27,132
Without proper wayfinding support, irrespective of the sophistication of the travel support, the user may not be able to deploy them efficiently or effectively.

224
00:28:28,154 --> 00:28:29,556
So that is about it.

225
00:28:29,772 --> 00:28:34,217
Wayfinding, and with that we can conclude the discussion on navigation techniques.

226
00:28:34,297 --> 00:28:40,703
The third interaction technique is interaction for system control.

227
00:28:40,723 --> 00:28:53,977
Now here the goal is to issue commands to change system state or mode, such as launching an application, changing the system settings, opening a file, and so on.

228
00:28:54,217 --> 00:28:59,222
Most of the regular operations that are associated with using any software.

229
00:29:03,016 --> 00:29:08,364
And correspondingly, there are several ways to perform those tasks through interactive methods.

230
00:29:08,404 --> 00:29:11,710
Different interaction techniques are there for system control.

231
00:29:12,231 --> 00:29:16,317
One can be physical controllers such as buttons, switches.

232
00:29:17,018 --> 00:29:28,416
Then we have graphical menus, adapted 2D menus suitable for 3D massive environments, one degree of freedom menus, 3D widgets.

233
00:29:29,948 --> 00:29:40,595
Then we can also have voice commands to perform the controls, gesture commands such as mimic gestures, symbolic gestures, sweeping gestures,

234
00:29:40,595 --> 00:29:50,489
sweeping gestures, sign language, speech-corrected hand gestures, whole-body interactions; all sorts of regular interaction modes can be deployed.

235
00:29:51,691 --> 00:29:58,858
We can also have tools such as physical tools, virtual tools, tangible interfaces, and we can

236
00:29:59,463 --> 00:30:13,416
combine multiple techniques to have multimodal interaction techniques for system control, where we can combine, say, voice with gestures or other sorts of interactions.

237
00:30:14,337 --> 00:30:22,504
So, with that we can conclude this part of the lecture, where we have covered the navigation interaction and interaction for system control.

238
00:30:22,604 --> 00:30:26,888
So, at the end, let us quickly summarize what we have discussed so far.

239
00:30:30,563 --> 00:30:35,749
We have learned the idea of design, essentially interface and interaction.

240
00:30:37,050 --> 00:30:46,862
Then we talked about design guidelines which can provide a starting point for a designer.

241
00:30:48,263 --> 00:30:58,495
Then we talked about interaction techniques typically used in immersive settings, primarily VR and also MR, not very important for AR.

242
00:30:59,116 --> 00:31:04,841
There we talked about object interaction, comprising object selection and object manipulation.

243
00:31:05,462 --> 00:31:10,927
We also talked about navigation interaction, comprising travel and wayfinding.

244
00:31:11,348 --> 00:31:20,817
And finally, we briefly touched on interactions for system control, which is more of a regular type of interaction with any software.

245
00:31:20,837 --> 00:31:25,802
In the next part of the lecture, we are going to talk about the design of the Majuli VR Tour.

246
00:31:26,102 --> 00:31:27,283
What are the elements?

247
00:31:27,668 --> 00:31:34,176
of design that are specific to the Majuli VR Tour and how we arrived at those design ideas.

248
00:31:35,958 --> 00:31:42,606
So, I hope you could follow the concepts and topics and that they were interesting to you.

249
00:31:43,347 --> 00:31:49,234
So, we will continue our discussion on design in the next part of the lecture as well.

250
00:31:49,955 --> 00:31:52,358
So, looking forward to meeting you in the next lecture.

251
00:31:53,659 --> 00:31:55,802
Goodbye, thank you, and happy learning.
